<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  iOS-only Info.plist overlay. Tauri merges `Info.plist` and, on iOS,
  `Info.ios.plist` from this directory into the generated bundle plist.

  It has to live here and not in `gen/apple/project.yml`, which is where the
  existing UIBackgroundModes entries are: `gen/` is gitignored (see the repo
  .gitignore, `**/src-tauri/gen/`) and is regenerated by `tauri ios init`, so
  anything set there is untracked and disappears on the next regeneration.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    `audio` is what keeps the wake word listening after the screen turns
    off. Without it iOS suspends the app a few seconds after the screen
    locks, AVAudioSession goes inactive and detection stops; the WKWebView
    is suspended even sooner, which is why detection had to move to Rust in
    the first place.

    Costs, deliberately spelled out because they are real:
      * App Review. Background `audio` on an app whose foreground purpose is
        not audio playback is a common rejection ("app does not play audible
        content in the background"). The reviewable justification here is
        voice-assistant wake-word listening, which Apple does accept, but it
        has to be explained in the review notes.
      * The orange microphone indicator stays lit while armed, and the app
        appears in Control Center's recording list. That is not suppressible
        and is arguably the correct user-facing signal for an always-on mic.
      * Battery. The chain runs ~12.5 inferences/second continuously.
    `fetch` and `processing` are repeated because this key replaces, rather
    than merges with, the array the generated plist already carries for the
    HealthKit background sync.
  -->
  <key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
  <array>
    <string>fetch</string>
    <string>processing</string>
    <string>audio</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    Shown in the iOS microphone prompt. The generated plist has a
    conversation-only wording; the wake word listens outside conversations,
    so the user has to be told that.
  -->
  <key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Seraphine usa o microfone para ouvir "Ei Seraphine" e para o modo conversa por voz. O audio e processado no proprio aparelho e nada e gravado ou enviado ate voce chamar por ela.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
